From: cube oakley <cube-oakley@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:07:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07/12] software_isp: Steadier contrast autofocus

Four refinements of the contrast autofocus, measured on another phone
with the same software ISP:

- Let the lens settle before sampling. Up to four requests are queued at
  the device, so the frames after a lens move were exposed at the old
  position: after a run that left the lens at 400, the first coarse
  sample at 0 read sharper (1.65M) than any real position (under 1.4M)
  and the scan settled at 100 instead of 400. Wait four samples after
  each step and six after the jumps to the start of a coarse or a fine
  scan.
- Fit a parabola through the best fine sample and its neighbours and
  move to its vertex, within half a step; the fine step alone left close
  subjects visibly soft.
- In continuous mode, follow the sharpness smoothed over about eight
  frames, take the reference from it once the lens has settled after a
  lock (15 samples), and scan again only once it has stayed below 60% of
  the reference for 15 frames. Hand shake blurs a few frames at a time,
  and a rescan whenever three frames read below 65% of the scan's best
  (noise-lifted) sample started a new scan every few seconds.
- Start a scan only once exposure has held for three frames, or after 20
  frames at most: the sharpness grows with exposure, so a scan run while
  exposure still rose preferred the positions it sampled last.

When manual focus hands back to continuous at the position the last
scan found, as around a still, resume watching instead of scanning
again.

[Derek Ross: combined into one change on top of the centre-window
 statistics; the 15-sample monitor delay after a lock is Robin Snyders'.]

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
